ion I posed  that as a question in a book and I say  maybe I was naive as an answer you know  maybe I just didn't understand about  homosexuality this is in the 70s and 80s  and in those days being gay in the 70s  meant that you were out at clubs doing  this is a stereotype but there was truth  to it and it's what filtered down to me  you know when you're crazy delivery  right and you're having doing nitrate  poppers your clubs all the time you're  having crazy wild sex but never a family  you're alienated from society I didn't  want that and then came AIDS and that  was that meant being gay was a death  sentence so I thought maybe it was you  know partly naive about what being gay  meant but the truth is to this day  having come out the other side of that  and seeing how all of these nightmares  about how terrible it would be to ever  admit what was going on how all of that  was a complete fabrication I still don't  understand why I and a lot of other  people would have this would rather  think of themselves as monsters in homo  section this is one of the places where  I think the book goes beyond just  talking about sexual orientation because  a lot of people are more willing to  think of themselves as monsters then as  a you know an example of a type in a way  but you write that in in the book it's  inexpressibly heartening to see today a  whole generation of homosexuals grow up  wondrously aloof from the disgust I took  for granted and I assume that's both  your own disgust as well as social  societal discuss it it's so it has  gotten better but it hasn't gotten fully  and go away you know people want to be  like other people they want to be normal  they want to have kids biological kids  with the person they love  and especially for I think boys there's  these elements of masculinity and a fear  of the feminine side and of what  homosexuality stands for and there's  worried about discrimination there's all  of that but for me the single best  biggest thing may 